  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Flight Strip Bay",
    "overview": "Spool is the bay an air traffic controller works from. Every open thread a contributor has on GitHub is a printed paper strip in a black plastic holder, and the strips sit in bays that answer one question: whose move is it. When the move passes, the strip slides out of one bay and into the other. The paper's colour says what kind of thread it carries, the holder's tab says its state, and the last box on every strip counts the days of silence, ringed in red grease pencil once it stalls.\n\nThe register is a working console, not a costume. The ground is a flat blue-grey night console with no texture, screws or gradients; the strips are the bright things on it, so the eye goes to the work and the room recedes. Type splits into two voices: Archivo for everything a person reads or acts on, and condensed Martian Mono for everything the strip printer sets (callsigns, numbers, times, statuses, counts, the faces). Motion is physical and sparse: strips slide between bays, fields print left to right, pencil marks draw on once. The night shift is the default; a day shift (light) is one switch away and keeps the same strips and holders.\n\nLetter-built faces, (^_^) and its family, are the one signature carried over from the earlier world. They are printed by the strip printer in a holder, react to the pointer and to each section, and live in the nav, the app header and the hero's position panel.\n\nConfirmed rejections: the dark dev-tool hero with a lit dashboard screenshot, the bento grid of feature cards, and the airport costume of the previous world (screws, bezels, hanging sign plates, departure-board signage, plane pictograms).",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Paper strips in black holders, coloured by thread kind, read left to right: callsign, title, last move, silence.",
      "Bays with a designator plate; amber lights only the bay that is your move.",
      "Red grease pencil marks silence past the limit: a ring round the count and a red holder tab.",
      "Flat blue-grey console in two shifts, night by default.",
      "Condensed Martian Mono for anything printed; Archivo for anything read.",
      "Strips slide between bays; fields print in; nothing else animates by default."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Paper Carries The Colour Rule",
        "body": "Saturated colour lives on strips, not on the room. Panels, bays and the console stay neutral in both shifts; if a surface that is not a strip, a plate or a mark turns colourful, it is wrong.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Amber Rule",
        "body": "Amber means the move is yours, or where you are. It never fills a button, a card or a panel.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pencil Means Stalled Rule",
        "body": "Red pencil appears only for silence past the limit and for closed-and-struck. Errors use Alert Coral text, not pencil.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Printer Sets Data Rule",
        "body": "Martian Mono is for what the strip printer would print: codes, numbers, times, statuses, counts and faces. Prose, buttons and headings are Archivo.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pin The Width Rule",
        "body": "Every element set in the print font also sets the width axis (`font-variation-settings: var(--print-wdth)`); width is not inherited from the family.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Holder Is The Depth Rule",
        "body": "Lift a thing by putting it in a holder, never by floating it on a shadow. No glows, no zero-offset coloured halos.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put every thread on a strip: callsign first, silence last, coloured by kind.",
      "Do say whose move it is with the bay, and the state with the holder tab and a pencil mark.",
      "Do keep the console flat and neutral in both shifts; let the strips carry the colour.",
      "Do set codes, numbers, times and statuses in condensed Martian Mono, and pin its width axis.",
      "Do make primary actions strips in holders that slide when pulled.",
      "Do label synthetic strips SAMPLE and live data LIVE, in printed tags."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't bring back the airport costume: no screws, bezels, hanging sign plates, flap tiles or plane pictograms.",
      "Don't fill buttons, cards or panels with amber; amber is for your move only.",
      "Don't use red pencil for anything but stalled silence and struck-through closed titles.",
      "Don't float things on glows or coloured halos; depth comes from holders.",
      "Don't put a kicker or eyebrow label above a heading.",
      "Don't set prose or buttons in the print font."
    ]
  }
}